\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Signed arrows preserve endpoints before incidence}
\date{2026-10-29}

\p{Reversing an oriented edge changes the sign of its difference, but
does not erase its endpoints. Fix a commutative unital ring #{k} and
two \vocabk{finite incidence presentations}{fdip-0002}
#{(V,E,s,h)} and #{(V',E',s',h')}. Equip them with weights
#{w:E\to k}, #{w':E'\to k}, scalars #{\mu,\mu'\in k}, and
retained subsets #{R\subseteq V}, #{R'\subseteq V'}.
Empty carriers, loops and distinct parallel edge labels are allowed.}

\p{Write #{C_2=\{+,-\}} for the abstract group with #{-\cdot-=+},
and #{\sigma:C_2\to k^\times} for #{\sigma(+)=1},
#{\sigma(-)=-1}. A \newvocab{signed endpoint arrow} consists of
bijections #{p:V\to V'}, #{q:E\to E'} and signs
#{\varepsilon:E\to C_2} such that
##{\begin{aligned}
\varepsilon(e)=+&:\quad s'q(e)=ps(e),\quad h'q(e)=ph(e),\\
\varepsilon(e)=-&:\quad s'q(e)=ph(e),\quad h'q(e)=ps(e),\\
w'q&=w,\qquad \mu'=\mu,\qquad pR=R'.
\end{aligned}}
The signs are abstract orientation data, even when #{1=-1} in #{k}.
An arrow with all signs #{+} is directed. For the
[real Gaussian backgrounds](fdip-000G), directed arrows are their exact
relabelings, with #{\mu=m^2}; positivity of mass then recovers #{m'=m}.}

\p{Composition applies the right arrow first. The composite with
#{(p',q',\varepsilon')} is
##{\bigl(p'p,q'q,\ e\mapsto\varepsilon'(q(e))\varepsilon(e)\bigr).}
Identity arrows have identity bijections and all signs #{+}. The inverse
of #{(p,q,\varepsilon)} has bijections #{p^{-1},q^{-1}} and the sign
#{\varepsilon(e)} at #{q(e)}. These operations make the declared objects
and arrows a groupoid: composition is associative, identities exist,
and every arrow is invertible, as for an [action groupoid](fgap-000P).}

\proof{
\p{For two arrows, zero, one or two endpoint exchanges occur according
as their signs are #{(+,+)}, #{(+,-)} or #{(-,+)}, and #{(-,-)}.
These give, respectively, the product signs #{+,-,+}. Thus composition
has the required endpoints. Also #{w''q'q=w'q=w} and
#{p'pR=p'R'=R''}; the scalar stays fixed. The inverse applies the
same sign twice, so restores endpoints and gives sign #{+}.
For three arrows either parenthesization gives the sign
##{\varepsilon''(q'q(e))\varepsilon'(q(e))\varepsilon(e).}
Associativity in #{C_2} and of the bijections proves associativity;
the stated identities and inverses satisfy both unit and inverse laws.}
}

\p{Let #{M=k^V}, #{N=k^E} and similarly #{M',N'}, with incidence
maps #{(df)(e)=f(h(e))-f(s(e))}. The induced ordinary linear maps are
##{\begin{aligned}
(Pf)(p(v))&=f(v),&P&:M\to M',\\
(Ja)(q(e))&=\sigma(\varepsilon(e))a(e),&J&:N\to N'.
\end{aligned}}
They are isomorphisms: #{P^{-1}f'=f'\circ p} and
#{(J^{-1}a')(e)=\sigma(\varepsilon(e))a'(q(e))}.
Evaluation at #{q(e)}, in the two endpoint cases, gives
##{d'P=Jd:M\longrightarrow N'.}
Both differences vanish on a loop; the endpoint condition still
remembers where that loop is attached.}

\p{For a \vocabk{left action}{fgap-0008} of a group #{\Gamma},
take actions #{p_g,q_g} on the vertex and edge sets. A
\newvocab{signed endpoint action} additionally requires
##{\varepsilon_1(e)=+,\qquad
\varepsilon_{gk}(e)=\varepsilon_g(q_k(e))\varepsilon_k(e).}
Thus the arrow assigned to #{gk} is the composite of those assigned to
#{g} and #{k}. The induced maps satisfy
#{P_gP_k=P_{gk}} and #{J_gJ_k=J_{gk}}. Indeed evaluation at
#{p_{gk}(v)} gives #{f(v)} on both sides of the first equation;
at #{q_{gk}(e)} the second has coefficient
#{\sigma(\varepsilon_g(q_k(e)))\sigma(\varepsilon_k(e))a(e)}.
Identity signs and maps give the identity operators. These are linear
representations on two distinct carriers; no faithfulness is assumed.}

\p{A weaker \newvocab{signed-incidence symmetry} requires the same
actions, abstract sign cocycle and weight preservation, but only the
incidence equation #{dP_g=J_gd}, instead of the endpoint equations.
It is an incidence intertwiner and suffices for
[operator transport](fdip-003D). It need not be a graph automorphism;
[loops and characteristic two](fdip-003C) distinguish the requirements.
The concrete [half-turn](fdip-000J) satisfies the stronger endpoint
conditions.}

\p{The left-action and equivariance conventions are
\citet{Definition 4.1, pp. 57-58}{milne2025group}.
\citet{Example 7.1(b), p. 101}{milne2025group} gives a permutation
representation over a field; the ring and signed endpoint construction
above is proved directly. The incidence convention in
\citet{Section 2.1, pp. 4-5}{spielman2009effective} concerns a connected
undirected positive-weight graph with a chosen orientation; the
endpoint-difference definition here also allows loops, parallel labels
and arbitrary coefficients.}

\p{Mathlib's \leanref{Representation.ofMulAction} sends the free
basis vector at #{v} to that at #{g\cdot v}.
\leanref{Finsupp.linearEquivFunOnFinite} identifies its coefficients
with functions on a finite set, yielding inverse precomposition for a
group action. \leanref{Representation.IntertwiningMap} bundles an
intertwiner between already supplied representations. Neither supplies
the endpoint equations or abstract sign cocycle above.}
